---
import Project from '../../../layouts/Project.astro';
import PageTitle from '../../../components/PageTitle.astro';
import Apparatus from '../../../components/Apparatus.astro';
import CiteBlock from '../../../components/CiteBlock.astro';
import FigureGrammar from '../../../components/FigureGrammar.astro';
import Factsheet from '../../../components/Factsheet.astro';
import JanghanRegisters from '../../../components/figures/JanghanRegisters.astro';
import j from '../../../data/janghan.json';
import { projects } from '../../../data/projects';
import { buildDate } from '../../../data/build';

const p = projects.find((x) => x.slug === 'janghan')!;
const rows = [
  { term: 'Sources', html: 'Four witnesses: the 1927 original scan (2005 digitisation); <span lang="ko">손종흠 외 편, 근대 기생의 문화와 예술 자료편</span> (<span lang="ko">보고사</span>, 2009); the Yonsei University serial-article catalogue; and the published bibliography.' },
  { term: 'Versions', html: 'Issue 1 corpus v3.3, frozen (v3.3.1 metadata-only amendment) · Issue 2 corpus v3.4, working layer · place list v1.1 · coded place evidence v1.2.' },
  { term: 'Datasets', html: 'Versioned JSON is published from the edition’s <a href="/janghan/methods.html">methods and data</a> page: textual items, editorial units, contributors, witnesses and coded place evidence.' },
  { term: 'Licensing', html: 'Project data and metadata CC BY 4.0. Source texts and page images remain with their rights holders and are not published here.' },
  { term: 'Build', html: `Generated from the frozen corpus; site built ${buildDate}.` },
];
---
<Project title="長恨 (1927) — Method and data"
  description="Method, coding scheme, dataset versions, limits, licence and citation for the reconstruction of 長恨 (Changhan, 1927).">
  <PageTitle title="Method and data"
    lede="How the reconstruction was made, what is versioned, what the coding does and does not establish, and how to cite it." />
  <section class="band band--flush">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="small muted breadcrumb"><a href={p.href}>← {p.shortTitle}</a></p>

      <section class="section first">
        <h2>How the contents were rebuilt</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <ol class="steps">
            <li><b>An inherited baseline.</b> 58 records across both issues, with approximate page ranges and no external check.</li>
            <li><b>Reading every candidate at source.</b> Three earlier passes had disagreed on how many articles were recoverable. Re-reading each at the page settled it, and turned up a two-part serialised novel that no inventory had recorded.</li>
            <li><b>Adding an independent witness.</b> The Yonsei catalogue was reconciled against the text. One inherited record proved to be a phantom; another was re-identified as a poem rather than an article.</li>
            <li><b>Recovering the page itself.</b> The 1927 scan showed that Issue 1 is set in two registers, a main upper block and a running bottom strip of short items, which the magazine's own table of contents marks and no prior inventory had recorded. Position is established for {j.issue1.register.upper + j.issue1.register.strip} of {j.issue1.items} Issue-1 items and left explicitly unknown for the rest, because it is not inferred from genre or content.</li>
            <li><b>Freezing Issue 1, then reconciling Issue 2.</b> Issue 1 is frozen at {j.issue1.units} editorial units, so every figure can be checked against the exact state of the evidence it came from. Issue 2 is reconciled to {j.issue2.units} but remains a working layer.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <JanghanRegisters />
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Coding</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Place mentions are coded per episode, a continuous stretch in which one place functions in one
            dominant way, under an eight-value function taxonomy, with explicit rules for relational anchors,
            rhetorical macro-geography and tabular data. Statuses distinguish counted mentions from gap-logged,
            logged-not-counted, excluded-evidence and false-positive records, so that decisions are
            reproducible rather than invisible. A 20% stratified audit of the Issue-1 coding measured recall at
            82% before correction and precision at 100%; all misses traced to one mechanical defect in search
            coverage, which was repaired and re-run.
          </p>
          <p>
            Issue-1 descriptive coding is complete. Issue-2 place and voice coding has not begun. Competing
            explanations for the observed patterns — genre composition, text length, page register, coding
            convention, retrieval error, editorial mediation, script register, and genuine expressive
            difference — remain under test, and none is presented as settled.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>A negative result worth recording</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Text-level measures of script density and politeness were computed for both issues and are retained
            in the dataset, but they are not published as an interface. Profiles of the same pen name proved
            unstable across the two issues: one moves from wholly plain to wholly polite, and an unsigned
            recurring column shifts just as far within the same genre. They therefore cannot serve as authorial
            fingerprints, and the reading mode is machine-assisted, agreeing with a human reading in nine of
            twelve checks. The instability is the result; publishing the table alongside a warning not to
            interpret it would invite exactly the reading the evidence does not support.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>At a glance</h2>
        <div class="measure"><Factsheet rows={rows} /></div>
        <Apparatus label="Counting conventions">
          <p>
            Counts are reported at the editorial-unit level and always name their level. The reconstructed
            counts are not claims about a historically true number: they converge with the other witnesses
            after reconciliation, and the residual disagreements are published rather than resolved.
          </p>
        </Apparatus>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Reading the figures</h2>
        <FigureGrammar />
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2 id="cite">Cite</h2>
        <CiteBlock
          citation={`Bilal, Mohd. “長恨 (Changhan, 1927): A Reconstruction of the Contents and a Register of the Attributions.” Digital humanities project, ${p.version}. The Academy of Korean Studies, 2026.`}
          licence="Project data and metadata are CC BY 4.0. Source texts remain with their rights holders."
          url="https://mohdbilaldh.github.io/projects/janghan/"
          buildDate={buildDate} />
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</Project>
<style>
  .breadcrumb { margin: 0 0 24px; }
  .first { margin-top: 0; }
  .steps { max-width: var(--w-measure); }
  .steps li { margin-bottom: 12px; }
</style>
